SDL3 change the ScummVM DOS port needs for its exit: the Sound Blaster
driver's WaitDevice waited for the card's interrupt handler to drain the
ring and never looked at the device's shutdown flag. A card whose
interrupts never come (BLASTER's D not the DMA channel the card uses: an
OPL3-SA set up by UNISOUND in 86Box, DMA requests on channel 3 with D1)
keeps the ring full, the audio thread stays in WaitDevice, and closing the
device (SDL_DestroyAudioStream, SDL_QuitSubSystem) waits on that thread
forever. WaitDevice now returns once the device is shutting down. The
fixed build defines DOS_SBShutdownChecked, which build-dos.sh looks for and
the port links against.

Base: sdl3-irq-code.patch and sdl3-cpuid.patch applied (~/opt/src/SDL3
branch dos-sb-shutdown = dos-486 2df4ec6 + fbd0d18). Apply after those two
(git apply skips this text):
  git -C SDL3 apply sdl3-irq-code.patch && git -C SDL3 apply sdl3-cpuid.patch \
    && git -C SDL3 apply sdl3-sb-shutdown.patch

diff --git a/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c b/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
index 74064b0..98f9dc7 100644
--- a/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
+++ b/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
@@ -172,6 +172,11 @@ static DOS_IRQ_CODE void SoundBlasterIRQHandler(void)
     }
 }
 
+/* Marks a build whose WaitDevice gives up once the device is closing: the
+   ScummVM DOS port refuses (and fails to link against) an SDL3 without it. */
+extern const int DOS_SBShutdownChecked;
+const int DOS_SBShutdownChecked = 1;
+
 // Wait until the ring buffer has room for one more chunk.
 // The audio thread keeps yielding so the game's main thread can run while
 // we wait. Because the ISR is steadily draining the ring, this only blocks
@@ -186,6 +191,11 @@ static bool DOSSOUNDBLASTER_WaitDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device)
     DOS_Yield();
 
     for (;;) {
+        // Without the card's interrupts the ring never drains: closing the
+        // device must not wait on that.
+        if (SDL_GetAtomicInt(&device->shutdown)) {
+            return true;
+        }
         // Available space = ring_size - (write - read).
         // ring_write is ours (audio thread only), ring_read is advanced by
         // the ISR. Read the ISR's copy so we see the latest drain position.
